\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Structural comparison between the traditional pivot translation approach (routing through Standard Bangla) and the proposed direct poly-dialectal mapping topology.}}{viii}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces End-to-end architectural workflow of the Poly-Dialectal Neural Machine Translation Framework, depicting five phases: source corpora integration from seven existing datasets and one novel manual corpus, the data processing pipeline, NMT model fine-tuning with DoRA, multi-directional translation paradigm, and evaluation with four standard MT metrics.}}{viii}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Evolution of Bangla regional dialect datasets and resources over time, highlighting the scale and dialectal coverage of the proposed corpus.}}{xii}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Dataset Composition and Source Provenance: A stacked horizontal distribution illustrating the severe class imbalance alongside the precise proportional contributions from prior corpora and manual augmentation efforts for each regional dialect.}}{xvi}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Cross-Dialect Parallel Coverage Matrix: An upper-triangular masked heatmap detailing the exact density of shared, parallel sentence pairs intersecting any two evaluated dialectal variants.}}{xvii}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Sentence Length Distribution: Structural variation across eight major Bangla regional dialects, measured in characters. Violin bodies illustrate the density, with dashed lines indicating the mean and solid lines denoting the median, highlighting the right-skewed nature of the dialectal corpora.}}{xviii}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Architectural comparison between Standard LoRA (left) and Weight-Decomposed LoRA (DoRA, right). While LoRA directly adds the low-rank update to the pre-trained weights, DoRA decomposes the weights into magnitude ($m$) and directional components, applying the low-rank update strictly to the normalized direction before re-scaling by the trainable magnitude.}}{xxi}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Parameter efficiency of DoRA fine-tuning across the three evaluated model architectures, demonstrating over 98\% reduction in trainable parameters compared to full fine-tuning.}}{xxi}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Comparative evaluation across experimental phases. BLEU, chrF++, and METEOR (higher is better) and TER (lower is better) are shown for all three models at 20 epochs and BanglaT5 at 100 epochs.}}{xxv}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Cross-dialectal BLEU score heatmap (BanglaT5, 100 epochs) spanning all 12 dialects. The full asymmetric evaluation matrix reveals translation directionality effects; top-performing pairs (BLEU $\ge 50$) are highlighted with dotted borders. Un-evaluated sparse pairs are marked with an em-dash (---). Darker shading indicates higher translation quality: morphologically proximate pairs (e.g., Mymensingh$\leftrightarrow $SCB) achieve the highest scores, whereas low-resource dialects (Rajshahi, Rangpur) exhibit uniformly lower scores.}}{xxvi}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Dual-axis chart illustrating the interplay between a dialect's linguistic proximity to Standard Bangla (x-axis), dataset volume (dashed line, secondary y-axis), and the resulting translation quality (grouped bars, primary y-axis).}}{xxvii}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Translation directionality asymmetry across six major dialects. Dialect$\rightarrow $SCB (green) consistently outperforms SCB$\rightarrow $Dialect (blue), with the BLEU gap indicated by annotations.}}{xxviii}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Distribution of translation errors across five linguistic failure categories, derived from manual analysis of 200 high-TER failure cases.}}{xxviii}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces BLEU and chrF++ as a function of training data volume for two DoRA configurations. Consistent improvement is observed, with diminishing returns beyond approximately 3,000 samples.}}{xxix}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Visual comparison of BLEU and chrF++ scores across baseline systems and the proposed approach.}}{xxx}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Multidimensional performance trade-off (Radar chart) demonstrating the advantages of INT8 quantization in reducing latency and memory footprint while preserving translation quality.}}{xxxi}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Overview and translation inference demonstrations of the deployed multi-dialect translation system: (a) main interface, (b) standard-to-dialect translation, and (c) direct dialect-to-dialect translation.}}{xxxii}{figure.caption.29}%
